WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.979 --> 00:00:05.730
What is mankind’s plan B for when poor old
Earth has ceased to be.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:10.360
Gone to meet its maker, rung down the curtain
and joined the choir invisible?

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.070
What happens when Earth is an ex-planet.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:17.370
Well, then it’s time to hop over the fence
and set up camp in our neighbour’s garden,

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.119
even if it’s a red and desolate wasteland.

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:26.039
Just over 78 million kilometres away on average,
Mars isn’t quite our closest neighbour,

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:29.500
that’s normally Venus, depending on its
position in its orbit.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.360
But Mars is definitely the best candidate
to sustain life.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.390
And we’re not talking about just going and
living in a caravan full of baked beans and

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:45.580
oxygen, we’re talking about a full civilisation,
out walking on the surface of the red planet.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:46.760
So is this really possible?

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.190
Could we actually make Mars into a place where
humanity could survive?

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:52.190
Could we breathe?

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:53.190
Could we grow food?

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.670
Are there any good bars?

00:00:54.670 --> 00:01:04.350
Today we look at the steps we would need to
take to become the Martians of the future.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:10.770
Now getting to Mars is something of a challenge
and it could take anywhere between 150 to

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.640
300 days, depending on space traffic.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.530
But this timeframe is perfectly manageable,
although a bit boring.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:23.870
The real issue is getting the huge volume
of stuff we’d need to colonise from the

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:27.810
surface of Earth out into space and then over
to Mars.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:34.590
Right now, it costs around $25,000 to get
just 1 kilogramme into orbit around earth.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:39.900
And although the money is obviously an issue,
think of all the resources, time and dedication

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:45.430
it takes to launch just one rocket into space,
never mind a continuous fleet of spaceships

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.890
travelling to Mars with supplies.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:52.070
Think how much effort it was to build the
International Space Station, and that’s

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:56.630
only 400 tonnes – that would barely carry
the toothpaste and peanut butter needed by

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.570
the Mars colonists.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:02.030
One proposed idea is to build a space elevator.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:06.840
It’s a surprisingly simple concept; you
just need a cable attached to the Earth that

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.550
extends out beyond the distance of a geo-synchronous
orbit.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:15.469
By that I mean the orbital distance where
a satellite rotates at the same speed as the

00:02:15.469 --> 00:02:20.959
Earth, so it stays in exactly the same position
out in space as the start of the elevator,

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:22.480
back on Earth.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.689
The cable would have a counterweight at the
end.

00:02:24.689 --> 00:02:28.939
The counterweight would swing around, like
a tetherball, and the centrifugal force would

00:02:28.939 --> 00:02:33.819
be enough to counteract Earth’s gravity,
which would mainly be acting on the lower

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:38.879
end of the cable, having much less influence
on the further away parts and the counterweight

00:02:38.879 --> 00:02:39.999
itself.

00:02:39.999 --> 00:02:44.769
This would keep the cable taught and now you
could run an elevator up and down it, using

00:02:44.769 --> 00:02:48.159
much less propulsion than you’d need for
a rocket.

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:52.939
Some estimates say that it would bring the
cost of putting 1kg of supplies into orbit

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:58.109
down from $25,000 to under $250.

00:02:58.109 --> 00:03:02.980
The obvious technical difficulty is building
a cable that would need to be around 100,000

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.560
km long.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.439
But with the development of materials such
as carbon nanotubes, it’s beginning to look

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:10.969
more possible.

00:03:10.969 --> 00:03:16.420
The cable cars, called climbers, would probably
use rollers to pull themselves up using friction

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:21.499
but there’s a limit to how fast they could
go up the cable, partly because of the strength

00:03:21.499 --> 00:03:26.739
of the material but also because as you get
higher, you are picking up a huge horizontal

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.299
speed, caused by the rotation of the earth.

00:03:29.299 --> 00:03:31.159
This is called the Coriolis force.

00:03:31.159 --> 00:03:33.659
You’d also need to power the climber.

00:03:33.659 --> 00:03:38.620
The journey would probably take 5 days, so
you’d either need a powerful nuclear reactor

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:43.800
or, the most likely solution, you could beam
the energy up to the climber using a laser

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:49.540
– meaning less weight being carried on the
vehicle itself, and more room for snacks.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:55.530
So if the elevator works, and all we need
to do to get material into space is listen

00:03:55.530 --> 00:04:01.519
to a few days of awful elevator music, the
next step is sending everything over to Mars.

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:04.200
This is where the moon could come in very
useful.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:08.900
The moon would never be able to have an atmosphere,
so it’s hopeless as a colonisation prospect,

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:15.989
but its lack of atmosphere is actually a huge
advantage because it means there is no friction.

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:21.660
The great science fiction writer Arthur C
Clarke discusses this in his non-fiction collection

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:22.790
Voices From The Sky.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:29.330
It’s worth pointing out that he also popularised
the idea of the Space Elevator in his 1979

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.790
novel The Fountains of Paradise.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:37.260
One idea that Clarke put forward is that you
could build a giant space gun that would fire

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:42.390
spacecraft down a track on the moon’s surface,
and fling them out into space.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:46.230
The craft would only need small propulsion
rockets to make adjustments for its entry

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.300
into Mars.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:51.640
This space gun wouldn’t be possible on Earth
because the friction of the atmosphere would

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:56.600
create too much heat, and you’d probably
kill about a million pigeons every journey,

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.150
as well as everyone on board.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:03.790
But on the moon, with a track just 30km long,
you could get the acceleration you need and

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:08.500
remain under the 10G that astronauts currently
experience when they leave earth.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:12.840
If you weren’t sending people, you could
use a much shorter track.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:18.260
The technology that we currently use for maglev
trains would work extremely well for this,

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:23.180
using a magnetic field to both float the vehicle
above the track, and to pull it forward.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:27.940
This is why Elon Musk and others are looking
into creating vacuum train tunnels for terrestrial

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.880
transport; no air means no friction.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:36.130
Speaking of Elon Musk, the amazing achievements
of Space X and the privatisation of space

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:40.930
travel is probably what will make this whole
colonisation project a reality.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:46.710
We’re still going to need rockets, at the
very least to build and maintain space stations.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:52.290
But when Falcon 9 landed on an automated drone
ship this April, after sending supplies on

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:59.320
to the ISS, it showed that we can finally
start reusing rockets, potentially turning

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:06.940
a $60 million trip, into just $600,000 for
fuel and maintenance.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:10.770
To get started on Mars, we’d need some pre-made
living spaces.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:15.760
They’d probably initially be something like
those put forward by the Mars One project

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.720
– a small series of contained compartments,
which you could link up.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:24.020
Therefore, it would be easy to just shut one
part off when you have a problem.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:29.510
And when it comes to keeping the astronauts
alive, well Mars has some very important features.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:34.910
Thanks to the work of our first Martian, the
hard working Curiosity rover, we now know

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:39.970
that there are all of the basic elements we
need in the ground and the atmosphere, so

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:43.940
we can create breathable air from what is
already there.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:48.690
There is ice in the soil that could be used
both as drinking water and to produce oxygen.

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:53.330
There’s nitrogen in the atmosphere so we
could make our favourite cocktail of earth-style

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:55.259
air, more or less.

00:06:55.259 --> 00:06:59.590
So that’s water and air covered, but what
about food?

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:04.500
Future colonisation attempts on Mars, such
as Mars One, plan to send a boatload, well

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:09.240
a spaceship load, full of fresh food to the
planet in advance so it will be waiting for

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.150
the colonists when the arrive.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:13.870
But when that food runs out…

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:16.530
well lets just say things get pretty grim.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:21.340
The Martians will live mostly on a diet of
algae and insects.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:24.880
Food will be produced hydroponically using
artificial lighting.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:30.190
Martian soil will be used to grow plants and
carbon dioxide will be provided from the Martian

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:31.200
atmosphere.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:36.020
The nutrients for the plants will come from
recycling human waste.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:37.050
Lovely.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:43.160
And if we move beyond just the basics of keeping
people alive, what about things like communication?

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:48.070
Even though you moved millions of miles away,
your grandma is still going to expect a call

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:52.520
at Christmas to explain why you aren’t married
yet and why didn’t you get a real job rather

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:55.570
than all this fancy space nonsense.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:59.720
Depending on the relative positions of Earth
and Mars, a message travelling at the speed

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:05.880
of light can take between 3 and 22 minutes
to go from one planet to the other.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:11.270
That means phone calls are out but texting
and internet messaging would be fine, so one

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:14.790
day the solar system will be cluttered with
a constant stream of emojis.

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:16.730
So, how’s Mars?

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:21.240
Desert icon, skull and crossbones, sad face.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:26.830
One other idea that Arthur C Clarke put forward
in Voices From The Sky is that you could use

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:32.710
lasers to beam much higher volumes of data
across space, if you make use of the moon

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:34.310
once again.

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:39.469
Lasers would be distorted by Earth’s atmosphere
but you could use the moon as a relay station,

00:08:39.469 --> 00:08:44.240
so messages could be sent that short distance
to the Moon by radio waves and then converted

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:48.230
to the more powerful laser form, and vice
versa.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:53.110
This means large data dumps of technical information
would be much easier and you wouldn’t need

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:55.519
to limit the coloniser’s communications.

00:08:55.519 --> 00:09:00.029
So the Martian folks would be able to send
all the selfies they want from Mars back to

00:09:00.029 --> 00:09:05.240
Earth; Yeah we get it, you guys are holding
a Mars bar on Mars, very funny.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:11.509
But now we come to the really important question;
can we make the whole planet liveable?

00:09:11.509 --> 00:09:15.279
Humans are actually pretty limited in terms
of the environment we can survive in.

00:09:15.279 --> 00:09:19.129
It’s not just the chemistry of the air but
also the temperature and the pressure that

00:09:19.129 --> 00:09:20.129
are important.

00:09:20.129 --> 00:09:25.060
Of course we could spend all our time in spacesuits
but it wouldn’t really be a normal life

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:28.639
and it would make Martian Next Top Model a
whole lot more repetitive.

00:09:28.639 --> 00:09:33.240
Besides, no one wants to spend all day in
a suit.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:37.820
The atmosphere on Mars is mainly carbon dioxide
and it’s at a far lower pressure than we

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:38.820
have on Earth.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:42.779
At our sea level, the pressure is about 100
kilopascals.

00:09:42.779 --> 00:09:47.569
To make life easier, we normally refer to
this as one atmosphere (101325 Pa).

00:09:47.569 --> 00:09:54.120
The lowest pressure us puny humans can breathe
in, unaided, is about 0.4 atmospheres.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:57.930
Anything under 0.2 would require us to wear
a suit.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:02.540
Mars is all the way down at 0.01 atmospheres.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:05.190
You really don’t want to be out in that.

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:08.970
In 1966, a technician was testing a spacesuit
in a vacuum chamber.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:12.220
The suit failed and there was a rapid loss
of pressure.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:17.600
The man quickly fell unconscious but he recalls
that the last sensation he had, before he

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:23.300
passed out, was feeling the saliva boiling
off his tongue; so without a spacesuit on

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:28.689
Mars you would rapidly boil alive before exploding
dramatically.

00:10:28.689 --> 00:10:33.050
To increase the pressure of the atmosphere,
we would face some major issues, one of the

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:39.300
biggest being that Mars lacks Earth’s powerful
magnetic field, called the magnetosphere.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:44.500
The Sun creates a solar wind; a stream of
matter, such as protons, electrons and alpha

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:47.680
particles, that are sent out in all directions.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:52.610
The part of the Earth facing the Sun, the
day side, is shielded from this particle breeze

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:57.440
by the magnetosphere and deflects much of
it away, keeping us safe.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:01.600
Some does enter through the poles and this
is how you get the Aurora, otherwise known

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:03.660
as the Northern or Southern Lights.

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:08.680
We believe Earth has this magnetic field because
of its liquid outer core.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:14.019
The moving molten metal deep inside the Earth
acts like a dynamo, just like how the spinning

00:11:14.019 --> 00:11:19.249
metal of a bicycle wheel can create an electric
field and power a light bulb.

00:11:19.249 --> 00:11:26.559
Mars once had this too eventually the planet’s
core cooled down and now its core is totally

00:11:26.559 --> 00:11:29.680
solid, so its magnetic field was lost.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.149
Without the protection of a magnetosphere,
the solar wind blows away much more of the

00:11:34.149 --> 00:11:35.860
atmosphere, thinning it out.

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:39.730
We have not yet come up with a good solution
to this problem.

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:44.670
What we do have are a variety of ways that
we could at least create the atmosphere once

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:48.120
again and raise the temperature of the planet’s
surface.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.759
The south pole of Mars holds a lot of frozen
carbon dioxide.

00:11:51.759 --> 00:11:57.309
If we could heat the surface a few degrees,
the CO2 would be released into the atmosphere

00:11:57.309 --> 00:12:03.050
and would then act a greenhouse gas, like
it does in a problematic way here on Earth.

00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:07.000
This would then warm the planet and you could
use simple life forms, like Phytoplankton,

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:10.830
to convert some of that carbon dioxide into
oxygen.

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:16.579
You could then slowly introduce more complicated
organisms and carefully build the eco system.

00:12:16.579 --> 00:12:21.259
As our knowledge of genetic engineering grows,
we will probably be able to alter the organisms

00:12:21.259 --> 00:12:24.129
to work better in each stage of the changing
environment.

00:12:24.129 --> 00:12:29.720
We’ll also be able to make 6 armed monkeys,
orange dolphins and dangerously fat mice,

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:31.749
just because we can and it’s funny.

00:12:31.749 --> 00:12:37.500
So how would we give the planet that initial
kick of energy to release all the CO2 and

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:40.300
create the desired greenhouse effect?

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:45.649
One idea put forward is to just bounce more
sunlight up to the pole with a series of giant

00:12:45.649 --> 00:12:46.709
mirrors.

00:12:46.709 --> 00:12:50.619
We could use a substance called PET (polyethylene
terephthalate) as you can stretch it very

00:12:50.619 --> 00:12:52.990
thin while maintaining a reflective surface.

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:57.610
It would be pretty slow though and I’m not
sure Mars would want to be staring at its

00:12:57.610 --> 00:12:59.330
awful red face in the mirror every day.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:05.690
A quicker method, although much more difficult,
would be to go asteroid hunting.

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:11.209
If we could find a few massive asteroids,
rich in ammonia since this is also an excellent

00:13:11.209 --> 00:13:16.699
greenhouse gas, and smack them into Mars,
then it would really speed up the whole process.

00:13:16.699 --> 00:13:20.490
But it would be a bit more complicated than
just sticking some cheese on a big stick and

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:23.199
hoping the asteroids are lured across the
solar system.

00:13:23.199 --> 00:13:28.960
We’d need to play a gigantic game of pool,
bouncing smaller asteroids into bigger ones

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:31.069
to put them on the path we want.

00:13:31.069 --> 00:13:35.319
But all of these challenges are just going
to make us push all that bit harder to find

00:13:35.319 --> 00:13:36.319
a solution.

00:13:36.319 --> 00:13:41.329
And every year, new technologies are emerging
and new ideas and approaches come from across

00:13:41.329 --> 00:13:42.329
the globe.

00:13:42.329 --> 00:13:46.029
Of course, we still haven’t thought about
the question of Martian society.

00:13:46.029 --> 00:13:49.869
Who knows what new religions and cults will
spring up on the red planet.

00:13:49.869 --> 00:13:53.310
And will it be a separate nation and have
a whole new set of laws of its own?

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:58.009
Will they have a Pappa Johns, we’re all
dying to know, but we’ll just have to wait

00:13:58.009 --> 00:15:01.160
and see what the next few decades bring.

